    {
      "short": "Add a queue",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Queues as shock absorbers",
      "body": [
        "Synchronous writes mean your database must absorb the <em>peak</em>. A <span class=\"term\">queue</span> (Kafka) changes the contract: producers get an instant ack, messages buffer, and workers drain them at a sustainable rate. The DB sees a smooth stream instead of a tsunami.",
        "The <span class=\"term\">backlog</span> became your key health metric: rising backlog = consumers too slow. Consumer parallelism is capped by <span class=\"term\">partition count</span> — more workers than partitions just idle."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "A queue buys time, not throughput — if drain rate stays below arrival rate forever, the backlog grows forever."
      },
      "metrics": {
        "users": 6000000,
        "rps": 72000,
